57
00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:48.740
About the office?

58
00:02:48.980 --> 00:02:51.000
The building is possessed.

59
00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:54.340
Why else would the people inside it act the way they do?

60
00:02:54.860 --> 00:03:02.120
Why would this delightful Pamela fall in love with that floppy-haired misfit, James Halpert?

61
00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:03.720
It makes no sense.

62
00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:06.440
People behaving in just absurd ways.

63
00:03:06.680 --> 00:03:08.280
The building is possessed.

64
00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:10.700
The building itself may be a demon.

65
00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:16.160
And Dwight Scruti himself may be some kind of dybbuk or homunculus.

66
00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:18.560
You think there's paranormal activity.

67
00:03:18.820 --> 00:03:23.400
In fact, several members of the office might be from hell.

68
00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:25.140
I am here merely to speculate.

69
00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:28.160
I am saying that perhaps there is a story underneath the story.

70
00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:30.440
Yes, this is a light comedy about co-workers.

71
00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:31.580
Hardy har har.

72
00:03:31.580 --> 00:03:35.720
But underneath it all, there may be something far more nefarious.

73
00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:36.700
Wow.

74
00:03:37.260 --> 00:03:39.340
Terry, how many episodes have you seen?

75
00:03:39.540 --> 00:03:40.480
Just the one?

76
00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:45.840
I am planning on starting to watch this show as soon as we finish this podcaSt.

77
00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:47.220
Because it is bewitching me.

78
00:03:47.260 --> 00:03:48.140
It has bewitched me.

79
00:03:48.580 --> 00:03:49.080
Okay.

80
00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:52.620
So you haven't seen any episodes, then?

81
00:03:52.940 --> 00:03:53.740
I have not.

82
00:03:53.740 --> 00:03:57.380
And have you listened to any episodes of Dark Air with Terry Carnation?

83
00:03:57.380 --> 00:03:58.820
No, you have not.

84
00:03:59.060 --> 00:04:01.100
So we're even, Stephen.

85
00:04:01.580 --> 00:04:01.740
Okay.

86
00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:03.160
Well, I don't think...

87
00:04:03.160 --> 00:04:03.820
I think we've...

88
00:04:03.820 --> 00:04:07.580
That show, Even Stevens, I have watched every episode of Even Stevens.

89
00:04:08.380 --> 00:04:08.860
Okay.

90
00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:12.980
Well, Terry, I think there might have been a miscommunication with your agent.

91
00:04:12.980 --> 00:04:13.480
Big fan.

92
00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:14.140
Huge fan.

93
00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:16.320
Thank you so much for having me, Office Women.

94
00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:20.200
And all of the listeners, thank you for enjoying this show and these two delightful

95
00:04:20.200 --> 00:04:25.240
young women as they share their inner torment with you on a weekly basis.

96
00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:27.440
Thank you, Terry Carnation.

97
00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:31.660
And I'm sure everyone will want to check out Dark Air.

98
00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:35.020
It's going to premiere April 1st on Audioboom.

99
00:04:35.220 --> 00:04:38.660
Terry, we'd love to have you back if you ever start watching the show.

100
00:04:38.920 --> 00:04:41.040
If you watch an episode, let us know.

101
00:04:41.240 --> 00:04:42.200
I really should do that.

102
00:04:42.200 --> 00:04:43.100
It looks fascinating.

103
00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:44.480
Well, thank you, Terry.

104
00:04:44.840 --> 00:04:49.820
There is one young accountant blonde woman who I find very intriguing.

105
00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:51.760
Oh, boy.

106
00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:52.500
Really?

107
00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:55.280
Fictionally speaking.

108
00:04:55.540 --> 00:04:57.020
Thank you for having me, Office Women.

109
00:04:57.320 --> 00:04:58.080
Thank you, Terry.

110
00:04:58.520 --> 00:04:59.240
Goodbye, Terry.

111
00:04:59.620 --> 00:04:59.880
Goodbye.

112
00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:02.580
Lady, he'd never seen the show.

113
00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:03.100
I don't.

114
00:05:03.140 --> 00:05:04.320
He had no idea what we were talking about.

115
00:05:04.520 --> 00:05:07.680
He also sounded suspiciously like Rainn Wilson.

116
00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:08.880
He really did.

117
00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:09.860
He really did.

118
00:05:10.220 --> 00:05:11.180
He called me Angel.

119
00:05:11.640 --> 00:05:13.160
Well, that was an adventure.

120
00:05:13.160 --> 00:05:14.800
We tried something new.

121
00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:18.440
That was exciting and confusing.

122
00:05:18.440 --> 00:05:23.460
I feel like we have some phone calls to make after we're done.

123
00:05:23.880 --> 00:05:25.580
Yeah, I feel like we're going to text some people.

124
00:05:26.260 --> 00:05:26.920
All right.

125
00:05:27.080 --> 00:05:28.360
Should we get into this episode?

126
00:05:28.580 --> 00:05:30.440
Should you actually read a summary?

127
00:05:30.680 --> 00:05:31.680
I'd be happy to.

128
00:05:32.380 --> 00:05:38.080
A lonely Michael and Dwight go to New York City to party with Ryan and meet ladies.

129
00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:43.620
In the meantime, the Scranton employees work late to avoid working over the weekend, but

130
00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:46.720
they wind up getting locked into the Scranton business park.

131
00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:49.520
And Terry did get this part right.

132
00:05:49.900 --> 00:05:51.700
Ryan may have a drug problem.

133
00:05:52.180 --> 00:05:53.180
Oh, man.

134
00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:53.720
Yeah.

135
00:05:54.220 --> 00:05:55.780
What are your fast facts?

136
00:05:55.780 --> 00:05:57.660
Here's a fast fact for you, Ange.

137
00:05:57.780 --> 00:06:01.660
I thought we could do a little breakdown of our guest star, Troy Underbridge.

138
00:06:01.980 --> 00:06:03.800
This is his second appearance on the show.

139
00:06:03.900 --> 00:06:06.320
You guys know he first showed up in the deposition.

140
00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:10.120
He is played by Noel Petock, and we reached out to him.

141
00:06:10.460 --> 00:06:11.300
Yes, we did.

142
00:06:11.380 --> 00:06:12.620
And Noel was so sweet.

143
00:06:12.620 --> 00:06:17.300
He sent in a bunch of audio clips, and he had some fun stories to share with us.

144
00:06:17.420 --> 00:06:21.460
Well, the first thing we wanted to know, we asked a lot of our guests, how did he get

145
00:06:21.460 --> 00:06:23.000
the job on The Office?

146
00:06:23.140 --> 00:06:24.520
And here's what he had to say.

147
00:06:24.520 --> 00:06:27.060
Hello, office ladies.

148
00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:28.800
This is Noel Petock.

149
00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:34.620
And I don't recall, really, Allison Jones ever having me audition.

150
00:06:34.640 --> 00:06:39.060
I actually started the whole process doing background through central casting.

151
00:06:40.220 --> 00:06:45.340
So I did, I think, just one episode where I did background.

152
00:06:45.340 --> 00:06:50.340
I was not really goofing off, but acted like I was kind of goofing off texting on a phone.

153
00:06:50.340 --> 00:06:57.720
I was kind of the posse of Ryan, EJ Novak's character, and had a good time doing it.

154
00:06:57.840 --> 00:06:59.580
I mean, just enjoyed the process.

155
00:06:59.940 --> 00:07:06.980
And later down the line, I had gotten a phone call from the assistant director, and she said

156
00:07:06.980 --> 00:07:13.140
she had spoken to Allison Jones and asked me if I'd like to appear on a couple episodes

157
00:07:13.140 --> 00:07:13.760
here and there.

158
00:07:13.760 --> 00:07:16.240
But I didn't actually audition.

159
00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:19.180
They just kind of offered me the role, and I gladly accepted.

160
00:07:20.200 --> 00:07:27.300
So, Angela, when I was a struggling actor, I did tons of background work in the hopes

161
00:07:27.300 --> 00:07:31.180
that someone would sort of discover me and give me a speaking role.

162
00:07:31.400 --> 00:07:32.520
It never happened.

163
00:07:32.520 --> 00:07:36.240
And it's super rare, but it did happen on our show.

164
00:07:36.420 --> 00:07:41.260
I mean, that's literally how Creed ended up becoming a series regular on our show.

165
00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:43.600
He started doing background work.

166
00:07:43.720 --> 00:07:46.420
And it's just something that I kind of like about our show.

167
00:07:46.780 --> 00:07:47.300
Yeah, me too.

168
00:07:47.400 --> 00:07:51.740
And this was also true for some of our warehouse employees that really just started as background

169
00:07:51.740 --> 00:07:52.220
characters.

170
00:07:52.420 --> 00:07:55.140
And then as years went by, they got to have speaking parts.

171
00:07:55.580 --> 00:07:57.820
And as an actor, that is so thrilling.

172
00:07:57.820 --> 00:08:02.780
But I love that Noel was there and that they went on to use him in several episodes.

173
00:08:03.320 --> 00:08:05.700
Well, that was due in large part to Mindy Kaling.

174
00:08:05.900 --> 00:08:09.400
And he actually talks about that in this next audio clip.

175
00:08:09.560 --> 00:08:10.980
You know, Mindy wrote this episode.

176
00:08:11.380 --> 00:08:12.920
So here's what he had to say.

177
00:08:13.360 --> 00:08:20.940
There is most definitely one person I'd like to thank who actually, I believe, wrote my character

178
00:08:21.780 --> 00:08:26.580
originally, and that is Mindy, who came up with the character.

179
00:08:26.840 --> 00:08:32.140
And she kind of came through in the office and ended up getting her own show.

180
00:08:32.420 --> 00:08:34.080
And Mindy is incredible.

181
00:08:34.340 --> 00:08:35.860
She's so sweet and smart.

182
00:08:36.080 --> 00:08:42.660
And I've got to thank Mindy for writing up this character, which was a blast to play.

183
00:08:43.660 --> 00:08:44.500
So sweet.

184
00:08:44.680 --> 00:08:45.540
I love that.

185
00:08:45.740 --> 00:08:46.460
Me too.

186
00:08:46.760 --> 00:08:50.120
Well, we have even more audio clips from Noel that we're going to share later in the show.

187
00:08:50.120 --> 00:08:51.960
And he is just the nicest guy.

188
00:08:52.100 --> 00:08:54.000
It was so nice to get to reconnect with him.

189
00:08:54.780 --> 00:08:55.020
Yeah.

190
00:08:55.480 --> 00:08:57.060
All right, my lady.

191
00:08:57.060 --> 00:08:59.300
What is your next fast fact?

192
00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:06.880
My next fast fact is a location breakdown because we got some fan questions from Abby,

193
00:09:07.180 --> 00:09:12.500
Lana, and Natalie, who would like to know, was this episode filmed in an actual nightclub?

194
00:09:12.780 --> 00:09:16.200
And Mariah Kay asked, are they different clubs?

195
00:09:16.440 --> 00:09:18.440
You know, the guys go to two different clubs.

196
00:09:18.660 --> 00:09:19.020
Yeah, yeah.

197
00:09:19.020 --> 00:09:20.800
Did we film in different locations?

198
00:09:20.800 --> 00:09:23.640
Or is it just two rooms in the same building?

199
00:09:23.980 --> 00:09:25.100
Well, I'll tell you guys.

200
00:09:25.620 --> 00:09:26.320
Tell us.

201
00:09:26.480 --> 00:09:32.960
In the episode, Michael and Dwight and Ryan and Troy, they go party at clubs in New York City.

202
00:09:33.380 --> 00:09:38.940
These club scenes were all filmed in downtown Los Angeles at the Edison nightclub.

203
00:09:39.460 --> 00:09:45.660
Now, the Edison nightclub is really big, and they had several different rooms, and they dressed them to look like two different clubs.

204
00:09:45.660 --> 00:09:52.260
So we only went to one location, and we knocked it all out, and they filled the club with all background actors.

205
00:09:52.260 --> 00:09:56.080
But, Ange, the Edison actually has a very cool history.

206
00:09:56.360 --> 00:09:57.140
The nightclub?

207
00:09:57.340 --> 00:09:57.500
Yes.

208
00:09:57.520 --> 00:09:58.380
Have you ever been there?

209
00:09:58.580 --> 00:10:02.120
Not only have I never been there, I've never heard of it, and I've lived in L.A.

210
00:10:02.300 --> 00:10:03.840
for over 20 years now.

211
00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:09.480
And if that tells you my hip factor, I had never heard of this nightclub.

212
00:10:09.660 --> 00:10:10.140
Me either.

213
00:10:10.360 --> 00:10:17.240
Angela, the only nightclub I've ever been to and have still ever been to is the one you took me to for my bachelorette party.

214
00:10:17.940 --> 00:10:18.560
That's it.

215
00:10:18.600 --> 00:10:19.520
That was so crazy.

216
00:10:20.080 --> 00:10:21.980
And I had never heard of that one.

217
00:10:22.100 --> 00:10:26.020
Remember, that was like a friend of a friend who was like trying to help me plan a night out for you.

218
00:10:26.220 --> 00:10:27.060
Okay, go on.

219
00:10:27.440 --> 00:10:29.800
Well, the Edison nightclub is in a historic building.

220
00:10:30.180 --> 00:10:35.760
It's called the Higgins Building, and it was the site of L.A.'s first private power plant.

221
00:10:36.040 --> 00:10:37.080
How about that?

222
00:10:37.360 --> 00:10:38.920
In downtown L.A.?

223
00:10:38.920 --> 00:10:39.260
Yeah.

224
00:10:39.780 --> 00:10:40.220
Wow.

225
00:10:40.400 --> 00:10:42.940
But here's the most interesting part, at least to me.

226
00:10:43.160 --> 00:10:46.780
They have a dress code because, quote,

227
00:10:46.780 --> 00:10:55.580
We are dedicated to a resurgence of old world style and a sense of romance that once dominated Los Angeles nightlife.

228
00:10:56.220 --> 00:11:01.420
So the nightclub, you have to look like fancy, like you got to wear suits and dresses and whatnot.

229
00:11:01.620 --> 00:11:03.480
Well, I think that's what they're hoping for.

230
00:11:03.820 --> 00:11:07.800
Basically, after 9 p.m., there are certain things you cannot wear.

231
00:11:07.960 --> 00:11:10.500
So here's the list of things you cannot wear.

232
00:11:10.720 --> 00:11:12.620
Why do I feel like this is going to rule me out?

233
00:11:12.940 --> 00:11:15.980
Oh, yeah, because listen to the first thing you can't wear.

234
00:11:15.980 --> 00:11:17.220
Athletic wear.

235
00:11:17.500 --> 00:11:18.560
Oh, no!

236
00:11:18.840 --> 00:11:20.320
All my athleisure wear!

237
00:11:20.600 --> 00:11:24.400
This includes shorts, jerseys, hoodies, and baseball caps.

238
00:11:24.640 --> 00:11:26.180
So Josh can't go.

239
00:11:26.340 --> 00:11:27.440
I can't go.

240
00:11:27.720 --> 00:11:29.900
You also can't have a backpack.

241
00:11:30.260 --> 00:11:30.520
Oh.

242
00:11:30.900 --> 00:11:38.580
You cannot wear flip-flops, sandals, short-sleeved shirts, or excessively torn or baggy jeans.

243
00:11:38.980 --> 00:11:40.180
Short-sleeved shirts?

244
00:11:40.360 --> 00:11:41.220
That's interesting.

245
00:11:41.220 --> 00:11:42.740
I mean, it gets hot here.

246
00:11:42.920 --> 00:11:43.420
Sorry.

247
00:11:43.820 --> 00:11:44.960
Don't know what to tell you.

248
00:11:44.960 --> 00:11:49.200
But if you would like to go to the location where we filmed Night Out, you can go.

249
00:11:49.880 --> 00:11:51.320
But dress spiffy.

250
00:11:51.460 --> 00:11:52.560
There you have it, folks.

251
00:11:53.000 --> 00:11:54.480
And lady, that's all I got.

252
00:11:54.480 --> 00:11:58.740
I know I normally do three fast facts, but Terry ate up a lot of our time, and I think

253
00:11:58.740 --> 00:12:02.180
we should just go to a break and come back and get into this episode.

