00:48:02.180 --> 00:48:04.200
And I slept really well that night.

995
00:48:04.340 --> 00:48:05.700
I don't think I had a concussion or anything.

996
00:48:05.820 --> 00:48:07.320
But I had the weirdest dreaMs.

997
00:48:07.600 --> 00:48:09.100
It was kind of exhausting.

998
00:48:09.440 --> 00:48:11.680
And yeah, don't try this at home, kids.

999
00:48:13.120 --> 00:48:18.180
Okay, Ange, I want to lead with it's not the same as being the person who's getting hit

1000
00:48:18.180 --> 00:48:19.300
in the face with the ball.

1001
00:48:19.560 --> 00:48:25.340
But it is not fun to be the person who has to throw a ball at their friend's face eight

1002
00:48:25.340 --> 00:48:25.920
times.

1003
00:48:26.700 --> 00:48:28.540
Eight balls to the face.

1004
00:48:28.540 --> 00:48:29.820
Yes, that sounds wrong.

1005
00:48:29.940 --> 00:48:30.880
But you know what I mean.

1006
00:48:31.100 --> 00:48:31.940
That's eight balls.

1007
00:48:32.480 --> 00:48:40.160
Well, I would throw it and I was trying to make it not that hard hitting her face.

1008
00:48:40.560 --> 00:48:45.800
And, you know, that was probably one of the reasons why we had to do so many takes because

1009
00:48:45.800 --> 00:48:51.680
eventually Ken got me aside and said, Jenna, you need to throw the ball in her face because

1010
00:48:51.680 --> 00:48:52.840
it's hitting her lightly.

1011
00:48:52.840 --> 00:48:56.340
And we just need one where you hit her in the face with the ball.

1012
00:48:56.520 --> 00:48:59.060
And I was like, oh, my gosh.

1013
00:48:59.400 --> 00:49:02.160
Well, you have to believe that Pam knows how to throw a football.

1014
00:49:02.380 --> 00:49:03.200
You have to sell it.

1015
00:49:03.320 --> 00:49:04.640
So you would have chunked it.

1016
00:49:05.400 --> 00:49:07.560
But man, Kate saying, you know what?

1017
00:49:07.680 --> 00:49:09.300
I want you to hit me in the face.

1018
00:49:09.520 --> 00:49:11.040
Thank you for providing a stunt double.

1019
00:49:11.200 --> 00:49:12.900
But I'll take this one for the team.

1020
00:49:13.540 --> 00:49:15.100
Ah, yeah.

1021
00:49:15.600 --> 00:49:16.560
Way to go, Jenna.

1022
00:49:16.780 --> 00:49:17.740
She had weird dreaMs.

1023
00:49:17.900 --> 00:49:18.260
Way to go.

1024
00:49:18.260 --> 00:49:18.860
I know.

1025
00:49:19.020 --> 00:49:19.600
I'm sorry.

1026
00:49:20.480 --> 00:49:25.680
Well, back in Da Club, number two, Michael thinks he's met the love of his life.

1027
00:49:25.880 --> 00:49:27.800
In fact, he wants to introduce her to his mom.

1028
00:49:28.280 --> 00:49:29.380
He's so happy.

1029
00:49:29.700 --> 00:49:33.900
And the camera operator kind of gives a little nod.

1030
00:49:34.140 --> 00:49:36.000
She's over chatting someone else up.

1031
00:49:36.200 --> 00:49:36.400
Yeah.

1032
00:49:36.600 --> 00:49:39.740
And I was like, oh, my gosh, here's the camera getting involved.

1033
00:49:40.200 --> 00:49:44.640
I feel like the documentary crew normally does this with Pam and not so much with Michael.

1034
00:49:44.640 --> 00:49:53.180
Yeah, it was nice that they sort of helped Michael not go too far down that imaginary relationship road.

1035
00:49:53.180 --> 00:49:55.360
We've already done that with the chair model.

1036
00:49:55.780 --> 00:49:59.420
So let's just, Michael, we'll dose a reality.

1037
00:49:59.740 --> 00:50:00.480
Take it easy.

1038
00:50:00.940 --> 00:50:01.400
Yeah.

1039
00:50:01.880 --> 00:50:07.780
Well, Ryan, meanwhile, is talking about an impending nuclear holocaust and jungle warfare.

1040
00:50:08.100 --> 00:50:08.480
Mm-hmm.

1041
00:50:08.780 --> 00:50:13.300
And then he takes to the dance floor and starts dancing like a crazy person.

1042
00:50:13.300 --> 00:50:15.920
And, like, shoves a girl and she shoves him back.

1043
00:50:16.060 --> 00:50:18.160
And now it's looking like it's going to be a brawl.

1044
00:50:18.660 --> 00:50:21.300
Michael's talking to his mom, as you do in the club.

1045
00:50:21.500 --> 00:50:21.780
Right.

1046
00:50:22.080 --> 00:50:25.300
And he's like, Mom, I got to go.

1047
00:50:25.500 --> 00:50:27.400
My friend's getting beaten up by some girls.

1048
00:50:27.880 --> 00:50:33.600
Well, have you know, Angela, we did employ a stunt person to do those fight scenes with Ryan.

1049
00:50:34.020 --> 00:50:35.520
So all good there.

1050
00:50:35.880 --> 00:50:36.280
Mm-hmm.

1051
00:50:36.500 --> 00:50:40.860
All of these shenanigans lead to the group getting escorted out of the club.

1052
00:50:41.860 --> 00:50:46.960
Troy makes a run for it, says, hey, don't take him to a hospital, and literally runs away.

1053
00:50:47.380 --> 00:50:47.640
Yes.

1054
00:50:47.820 --> 00:50:51.880
Well, we had to ask Noel what it was like to just shoot all of these club scenes.

1055
00:50:52.020 --> 00:50:53.440
This was really an epic shoot.

1056
00:50:53.620 --> 00:50:55.300
And here's what he had to say.

1057
00:50:56.300 --> 00:50:59.980
Shooting the nightclub scenes were fun to do.

1058
00:50:59.980 --> 00:51:07.600
So I don't really remember any kind of improvising during those few scenes in the Night Out episode.

1059
00:51:07.900 --> 00:51:09.880
There was no really breaking character.

1060
00:51:10.140 --> 00:51:11.520
I tried to stay professional.

1061
00:51:11.880 --> 00:51:19.600
And the only kind of stuff that was funny during the scenes was the scenes with BJ and Steve,

1062
00:51:19.600 --> 00:51:23.560
where he says, why won't the girls let me dance?

1063
00:51:25.320 --> 00:51:30.020
And I would get a lot of recognition from my don't take him to a hospital line,

1064
00:51:30.180 --> 00:51:32.020
where I'm like running down the dark alley.

1065
00:51:32.780 --> 00:51:40.720
But another fun story was later, there was a day where we ended up shooting quite late,

1066
00:51:40.720 --> 00:51:43.300
and they had actually dismissed Steve Carell.

1067
00:51:43.520 --> 00:51:48.340
And I was left last on set shooting a monologue at past midnight.

1068
00:51:48.700 --> 00:51:51.020
And that day, I had a 7 a.m.

1069
00:51:51.100 --> 00:51:51.300
call.

1070
00:51:51.400 --> 00:51:57.820
So I was dead tired shooting this monologue over and over again, which we did eventually get.

1071
00:51:57.820 --> 00:52:00.520
But leaving at 1 a.m.

1072
00:52:00.520 --> 00:52:03.460
or so was quite interesting that day.

1073
00:52:04.160 --> 00:52:06.700
Well, I really relate to what Noel said here,

1074
00:52:06.820 --> 00:52:10.540
because many times I had talking heads at the end of the day,

1075
00:52:10.660 --> 00:52:15.240
and you're so tired, and half of the cast has gone home, and you're trying to focus.

1076
00:52:15.540 --> 00:52:19.680
But yes, if you go to the deleted scenes, there's a talking head by Troy,

1077
00:52:19.920 --> 00:52:24.380
where he totally denies his role in helping Ryan get drugs.

1078
00:52:26.240 --> 00:52:28.780
Well, we were also curious, Angela, to hear,

1079
00:52:28.980 --> 00:52:33.840
how often does Noel get recognized from being on The Office?

1080
00:52:33.880 --> 00:52:36.960
Because he was in several episodes, and here's what he had to say.

1081
00:52:37.740 --> 00:52:40.520
Oh, the big question, do I get recognized?

1082
00:52:40.940 --> 00:52:43.860
But I do get recognized here and there at Starbucks,

1083
00:52:43.860 --> 00:52:49.020
or even during outings with my family when I'm just going to dinner at a restaurant.

1084
00:52:49.320 --> 00:52:52.060
A busboy will even say, weren't you on The Office?

1085
00:52:52.060 --> 00:52:54.020
Which is just kind of a crack up to me.

1086
00:52:54.320 --> 00:53:00.340
But I think the furthest recognition I got was a vacation trip to Hawaii,

1087
00:53:00.920 --> 00:53:05.440
and a young lady in a store was actually chatting with my mom,

1088
00:53:05.440 --> 00:53:10.040
and asked if her son was on The Office, and she was all excited,

1089
00:53:10.320 --> 00:53:13.140
and actually asked me to come back and get a picture with her,

1090
00:53:13.220 --> 00:53:14.780
which is a blast to do.

1091
00:53:14.920 --> 00:53:15.920
It's just kind of funny to me.

1092
00:53:15.920 --> 00:53:20.440
But I'm always open to signing autographs or taking pictures with fans.

1093
00:53:20.440 --> 00:53:22.260
I actually quite enjoy it.

1094
00:53:22.620 --> 00:53:26.420
And I just loved everything about The Office.

1095
00:53:26.420 --> 00:53:31.520
It was so much fun to do, but I always loved being around everybody.

1096
00:53:32.640 --> 00:53:33.320
So sweet.

1097
00:53:33.740 --> 00:53:34.760
So sweet.

1098
00:53:35.200 --> 00:53:38.300
Back in the lobby, Hank is still not there.

1099
00:53:38.520 --> 00:53:39.940
He hasn't even left his house yet.

1100
00:53:40.200 --> 00:53:43.860
I would dare to say Hank is in no hurry to go get them.

1101
00:53:43.860 --> 00:53:47.280
Meanwhile, in the lobby, Toby is cracking the jokes,

1102
00:53:47.640 --> 00:53:49.060
feeling pretty good about himself.

1103
00:53:49.580 --> 00:53:49.780
Yeah.

1104
00:53:50.360 --> 00:53:53.080
Pam is laughing at Toby's jokes.

1105
00:53:53.580 --> 00:53:56.740
And he is so pleased with himself that without knowing it,

1106
00:53:56.880 --> 00:54:02.920
he puts his hand on Pam's knee and then, like, kind of strokes it with one finger,

1107
00:54:03.100 --> 00:54:04.040
like his index finger.

1108
00:54:04.140 --> 00:54:06.240
He kind of does a little swipey-swipey back and forth.

1109
00:54:06.440 --> 00:54:09.080
And everyone clocks it.

1110
00:54:09.340 --> 00:54:11.820
It is so cringy and awkward.

1111
00:54:12.660 --> 00:54:18.200
So then Toby announces, very suddenly, that he's moving to Costa Rica.

1112
00:54:18.620 --> 00:54:22.120
He runs out the door, hops the fence, and jogs home.

1113
00:54:22.420 --> 00:54:23.480
Hops the fence?

1114
00:54:23.640 --> 00:54:26.500
This is no hop the fence type of fence.

1115
00:54:26.720 --> 00:54:28.020
I rewatched this.

1116
00:54:28.160 --> 00:54:30.860
This is a scaling over a wall type of fence.

1117
00:54:31.020 --> 00:54:32.820
It is really tall.

1118
00:54:33.140 --> 00:54:34.600
When did Toby get so athletic?

1119
00:54:34.880 --> 00:54:36.360
I also did screen grab.

1120
00:54:36.500 --> 00:54:37.780
You can clearly see the stuntman.

1121
00:54:37.780 --> 00:54:38.480
All right.

1122
00:54:38.580 --> 00:54:42.380
Well, we got some fan mail about that from Emily, Abby, Christy, and Ellie.

1123
00:54:42.820 --> 00:54:45.660
Was that really Paul Lieberstein jumping over the fence?

1124
00:54:46.220 --> 00:54:46.740
No.

1125
00:54:46.980 --> 00:54:48.600
I reached out to Paul.

1126
00:54:48.820 --> 00:54:52.100
And Paul told me that he was willing to do it.

1127
00:54:52.220 --> 00:54:53.640
He offered to try.

1128
00:54:53.860 --> 00:54:57.220
But they explained that we would have to shoot the scene multiple times,

1129
00:54:57.420 --> 00:54:59.900
which increased the possibility for injury.

1130
00:55:00.100 --> 00:55:02.500
And so they insisted on hiring a stunt person.

1131
00:55:02.500 --> 00:55:07.740
He also said, quote, obviously, I'm a guy who does his own stunts,

1132
00:55:07.860 --> 00:55:09.320
but I had to put the show firSt.

1133
00:55:11.780 --> 00:55:13.980
Well, Paul, I get it.

1134
00:55:14.100 --> 00:55:15.140
You wanted to do it.

1135
00:55:15.260 --> 00:55:16.160
They said no.

1136
00:55:16.160 --> 00:55:18.020
So you had to do what they said.

1137
00:55:18.180 --> 00:55:19.780
I want to say something, Angela.

1138
00:55:20.000 --> 00:55:24.680
I remember that we shot this scene very, very late on a Friday night.

1139
00:55:24.840 --> 00:55:26.800
It was like 2 a.m.

1140
00:55:27.200 --> 00:55:31.220
And I think that that also increases the risk of injury.

1141
00:55:31.220 --> 00:55:35.880
I think you should not attempt to do your own stunts at 2 a.m.

1142
00:55:36.140 --> 00:55:37.580
So I think it was a wise move.

1143
00:55:37.980 --> 00:55:42.620
Especially one that involves basically scaling something that's one story high.

1144
00:55:42.840 --> 00:55:44.140
I mean, I'm exaggerating.

1145
00:55:44.300 --> 00:55:45.700
But it's a really tall fence.

1146
00:55:45.760 --> 00:55:46.900
It really is.

1147
00:55:47.080 --> 00:55:50.180
He could have easily twisted an ankle or broken an arm.

1148
00:55:50.540 --> 00:55:53.660
We had a fan theory by Carolyn G.

1149
00:55:54.360 --> 00:55:57.740
Since Toby jumps the fence with such apparent ease,

1150
00:55:57.740 --> 00:56:02.140
it shows that he's someone who might be able to escape the police.

1151
00:56:02.820 --> 00:56:06.760
Is this added evidence that Toby is the Scranton Strangler?

1152
00:56:07.000 --> 00:56:08.860
I asked Paul about this.

1153
00:56:09.200 --> 00:56:10.780
His response was,

1154
00:56:11.360 --> 00:56:12.120
Sounds smart.

1155
00:56:12.740 --> 00:56:13.840
Mm-hmm.

1156
00:56:14.420 --> 00:56:15.380
Dun-dun-dun.

1157
00:56:15.580 --> 00:56:17.840
Perhaps if you were the Scranton Strangler,

1158
00:56:18.060 --> 00:56:21.140
you might have to set up residence outside of the country.

1159
00:56:21.820 --> 00:56:22.340
Wow.

1160
00:56:22.580 --> 00:56:23.740
That's a good point.

1161
00:56:23.980 --> 00:56:24.640
Just saying.

1162
00:56:25.300 --> 00:56:26.340
Well, hold up.

1163
00:56:26.480 --> 00:56:27.120
What's happening?

1164
00:56:27.300 --> 00:56:28.760
The cleaning crew has arrived.

1165
00:56:28.920 --> 00:56:29.400
Yes.

1166
00:56:29.680 --> 00:56:31.620
And the cleaning crew lets them out.

1167
00:56:31.960 --> 00:56:32.920
Everyone is thrilled.

1168
00:56:33.220 --> 00:56:34.080
Still no Hank.

1169
00:56:34.540 --> 00:56:35.940
Oh, well, we'll get to that.

1170
00:56:36.360 --> 00:56:36.700
Mm-hmm.

1171
00:56:36.940 --> 00:56:38.680
Meanwhile, back in New York,

1172
00:56:38.900 --> 00:56:41.020
the guys arrive at Ryan's apartment,

1173
00:56:41.020 --> 00:56:42.300
and Dwight and Michael,

1174
00:56:42.460 --> 00:56:44.600
they're going to crash there for the night.

1175
00:56:44.600 --> 00:56:46.520
They're kind of fighting over the sofa.

1176
00:56:46.860 --> 00:56:48.880
Well, I want to say something about this apartment.

1177
00:56:48.880 --> 00:56:51.720
It looks like a one-bedroom studio,

1178
00:56:51.720 --> 00:56:53.980
and I really appreciated that

1179
00:56:53.980 --> 00:56:58.540
because I always thought the apartments on the show Friends were ridiculous.

1180
00:56:59.140 --> 00:57:00.500
I lived in New York.

1181
00:57:00.700 --> 00:57:02.300
No one could afford those things.

1182
00:57:02.580 --> 00:57:02.920
No.

1183
00:57:03.100 --> 00:57:06.600
So I appreciated that Ryan had a very modest apartment in New York.

1184
00:57:06.800 --> 00:57:08.240
I mean, it was very messy,

1185
00:57:08.300 --> 00:57:11.120
but I'd like to point out at 18 minutes, 59 seconds,

1186
00:57:11.460 --> 00:57:14.120
he's got an enormous flat-screen TV.

1187
00:57:14.700 --> 00:57:15.200
Mm-hmm.

1188
00:57:15.200 --> 00:57:15.900
Yeah.

1189
00:57:16.220 --> 00:57:19.120
I wish we could have had a moment where Michael clocks that.

1190
00:57:19.700 --> 00:57:20.300
Me too.

1191
00:57:20.660 --> 00:57:22.240
And, like, maybe it was on an arm,

1192
00:57:22.300 --> 00:57:22.980
and he pulled it out,

1193
00:57:23.040 --> 00:57:24.700
and it actually went further than two inches.

1194
00:57:24.900 --> 00:57:25.880
That would have been amazing.

1195
00:57:26.600 --> 00:57:29.400
Well, Dwight is going to sing Ryan a lullaby,

1196
00:57:29.940 --> 00:57:32.180
and we had a fan question about it, Angela,

1197
00:57:32.340 --> 00:57:33.280
from Cameron D.

1198
00:57:33.460 --> 00:57:34.300
and Joe S.

1199
00:57:34.500 --> 00:57:38.480
Did the writers make up the lullaby that Dwight sings to Ryan,

1200
00:57:38.660 --> 00:57:40.100
or is it real?

1201
00:57:40.480 --> 00:57:42.540
I think Rain improvised that moment.

1202
00:57:42.680 --> 00:57:43.440
That's my guess.

1203
00:57:44.140 --> 00:57:45.800
It's so much better than that.

1204
00:57:46.360 --> 00:57:46.580
What?

1205
00:57:46.800 --> 00:57:48.960
This is a real lullaby?

1206
00:57:49.380 --> 00:57:54.220
And our script supervisor, Veda, taught it to Rain.

1207
00:57:54.860 --> 00:57:55.420
Amazing.

1208
00:57:55.720 --> 00:57:56.060
Yes.

1209
00:57:56.200 --> 00:57:59.680
It was one that her Austrian grandmother used to sing to her.

1210
00:57:59.980 --> 00:58:02.860
But here is something that Mindy revealed in an interview.

1211
00:58:03.100 --> 00:58:05.320
She said they never found out what the words meant.

1212
00:58:05.560 --> 00:58:09.680
Veda just wrote it out phonetically for Rain right before the scene.

1213
00:58:09.680 --> 00:58:13.980
So he could be singing, like, some creepy stuff.

1214
00:58:14.140 --> 00:58:14.800
We don't know.

1215
00:58:15.220 --> 00:58:15.480
Okay.

1216
00:58:16.040 --> 00:58:21.460
As Ryan is going to sleep, he tells them he thinks his friend Troy might have a drug problem,

1217
00:58:21.460 --> 00:58:24.140
and I think we all know who he's talking about.

1218
00:58:24.820 --> 00:58:29.460
Michael gives him horrible advice, and then looks to Cameron and says,

1219
00:58:29.820 --> 00:58:30.580
best night ever.

1220
00:58:31.100 --> 00:58:31.540
Yeah.

1221
00:58:31.840 --> 00:58:36.340
Michael has a talking head where he said, you know, this whole time in his life, it's not

1222
00:58:36.340 --> 00:58:38.680
the horniness, it's the loneliness.

1223
00:58:39.340 --> 00:58:41.780
But how could he be lonely when he's with his boys?

1224
00:58:42.220 --> 00:58:44.960
Like a famous person once said, boys on the side.

1225
00:58:45.420 --> 00:58:47.440
Let's hear it for the boys.

1226
00:58:50.340 --> 00:58:55.840
Well, I would not want to be Jim Helpert on Monday because Hank shows up, sees that none

1227
00:58:55.840 --> 00:58:59.360
of them are there, and goes, son of a bitch.

1228
00:59:00.060 --> 00:59:05.120
You know, I did have to wonder, Angela, did Hank not notice all of their cars in the parking

1229
00:59:05.120 --> 00:59:06.980
lot when he chained it for the night?

1230
00:59:07.000 --> 00:59:10.740
Or was he just like, listen, these guys didn't give me a bonus.

1231
00:59:10.740 --> 00:59:11.360
I don't know.

1232
00:59:11.480 --> 00:59:11.980
I'm leaving.

1233
00:59:12.320 --> 00:59:13.800
There's probably a bit of that.

1234
00:59:14.040 --> 00:59:15.100
They didn't call me.

1235
00:59:15.220 --> 00:59:16.160
They didn't give me a bonus.

1236
00:59:16.760 --> 00:59:17.700
I'm out of here.

1237
00:59:17.980 --> 00:59:18.880
I'm out of here.

1238
00:59:19.100 --> 00:59:20.040
Good luck, a-holes.

1239
00:59:21.760 --> 00:59:23.960
Well, that was night out, you guys.

1240
00:59:24.220 --> 00:59:27.020
Thank you so much for sending in your questions and your comments.

1241
00:59:27.380 --> 00:59:27.580
